        "sections": [
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).</p>",
            "readable_text": "As we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>The same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let&#x27;s compute the value of</p>",
            "readable_text": "The same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p><span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS.  First, we compute the exponents:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9}  = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}]</span> </p>",
            "readable_text": "To evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n[MATH: (-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9} = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Then, we multiply:\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-2) \\times 9 = -18]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Then, we multiply:\n[MATH: (-2) \\times 9 = -18]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          },
          {
            "section_type": "content",
            "normalized_html": "<p>Therefore,\n<span class=\"math\">[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.]</span></p>",
            "readable_text": "Therefore,\n[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.]",
            "images": [],
            "free_entry_blanks": [],
            "select_lists": []
          }
        ],
        "images": [],
        "free_entry_blanks": [],
        "select_lists": [],
        "readable_text": "Content:\nAs we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nContent:\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2.]\n\nContent:\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n[MATH: (-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9} = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}]\n\nContent:\nThen, we multiply:\n[MATH: (-2) \\times 9 = -18]\n\nContent:\nTherefore,\n[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.]"
      },
