    "readable_text": "Item 1 \u2014 Tutorial: Introduction\n\nContent:\nAs we already know, to evaluate numerical expressions with a mix of different operations, we must follow the order of operations (PEMDAS).\n\nContent:\nThe same rules apply to numerical expressions with negative numbers. For example, let's compute the value of\n\nContent:\n[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2.]\n\nContent:\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n[MATH: (-2)\\,\\times\\overbrace{(-3)^2}^{\\large\\color{blue} 9} = (-2) \\times {\\color{blue} 9}]\n\nContent:\nThen, we multiply:\n[MATH: (-2) \\times 9 = -18]\n\nContent:\nTherefore,\n[MATH: (-2) \\times (-3)^2 = -18.]\n\n---\n\nItem 2 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions With Stand-Alone Exponents\n\nExample Prompt:\nCalculate [MATH: (-3)^3 \\div 6].\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we compute the exponents:\n\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{3}_(\u23df)_(\u2212 27) \u00f7 6 = (\u2212 27) \u00f7 6]\n\nThen, we divide:\n\n[MATH: (\u2212 27) \u00f7 6|=; ((\u2212 27))/(6)|=; \u2212 (27)/(6)|=; \u2212 (27 \u00f7 3)/(6 \u00f7 3)|=; \u2212 (9)/(2)]\n\n---\n\nItem 3 \u2014 Question 1\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{3} + 4^{3} =]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 55]\nb. [MATH: 15]\nc. [MATH: 3]\nd. [MATH: 81]\ne. [MATH: 37]\n\n---\n\nItem 4 \u2014 Question 2\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 4 \u00d7 (\u2212 2)^{4} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 5 \u2014 Question 3\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: 6 \u00f7 3 + (\u2212 3)^{3} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 6 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions With Exponents Outside Parentheses\n\nExample Prompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: (1-4)^2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{12}.]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, we perform the operation inside the parentheses:\n\n[MATH: (1 \u2212 4_(\u23df)_(\u2212 3))^{2} \u00d7 (1)/(12)|= (\u2212 3)^{2} \u00d7 (1)/(12)]\n\nNext, we compute the exponents:\n\n[MATH: (\u2212 3)^{2}_(\u23df)_(9) \u00d7 (1)/(12)|= 9 \u00d7 (1)/(12)]\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n[MATH: 9 \u00d7 (1)/(12)|=; (9)/(1) \u00d7 (1)/(12)|=; (9 \u00d7 1)/(1 \u00d7 12)|=; (9)/(12)|=; (9 \u00f7 3)/(12 \u00f7 3)|=; (3)/(4)]\n\n---\n\nItem 7 \u2014 Question 4\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (1 \u2212 (\u2212 3))^{3} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 8 \u2014 Question 5\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: (\u2212 1 \u2212 2)^{3}.]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: \u2212 27]\nb. [MATH: \u2212 1]\nc. [MATH: 8]\nd. [MATH: 1]\ne. [MATH: \u2212 9]\n\n---\n\nItem 9 \u2014 Question 6\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: (4 \u00f7 (\u2212 2) + 5)^{4} =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 10 \u2014 Example: Evaluating Expressions With Exponents Inside Parentheses\n\nExample Prompt:\nWhat is the value of [MATH: \\left( 5 \\div (-2)^2 \\right) \\times \\left( -\\dfrac{4}{5} \\right).]\n\nContent:\nEXPLANATION\n\nExplanation:\nTo evaluate this expression, we use PEMDAS. First, consider the expression inside the parenthesis:\n\n[MATH: 5 \u00f7 (\u2212 2)^{2}]\n\nApplying PEMDAS to this expression, we obtain:\n\n[MATH: 5 \u00f7 (\u2212 2)^{2}|=; 5 \u00f7 4|=; (5)/(4)]\n\nFinally, we multiply:\n\n[MATH: (5 \u00f7 (\u2212 2)^{2}^(\u23de)^((5)/(4))) \u00d7 (\u2212 (4)/(5))|=; (5)/(4) \u00d7 (\u2212 (4)/(5))|=; \u2212 (5)/(4) \u00d7 (4)/(5)|=; \u2212 (5 \u00d7 4)/(4 \u00d7 5)|=; \u2212 (5 \u00d7 4)/(4 \u00d7 5)|=; \u2212 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 11 \u2014 Question 7\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((\u2212 3)^{2} + (\u2212 2)^{4}) \u00d7 (\u2212 (1)/(5)) =][FREE_ENTRY 1]\n\n---\n\nItem 12 \u2014 Question 8\n\nPrompt:\nEvaluate [MATH: (\u2212 (1)/(3)) \u00f7 ((\u2212 4)^{3} \u00f7 8).]\n\nChoices:\na. [MATH: 12]\nb. [MATH: (8)/(3)]\nc. [MATH: \u2212 (5)/(24)]\nd. [MATH: \u2212 3]\ne. [MATH: (1)/(24)]\n\n---\n\nItem 13 \u2014 Question 9\n\nPrompt:\n[MATH: ((\u2212 4)^{2} + 5 \u00d7 2) \u2212 ((\u2212 2)^{3} \u00f7 (\u2212 4)) =][FREE_ENTRY 1]"
